 <FONT face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="1" ><B></b></font>
<p><FONT face="verdana, tahoma" size="2" >In the simplest form, and easiest way to state it, its not <b>what</b> I believe in, but what I <b>dont</b> believe in. I dont belive in hate, punishment, suffering, or judgement (in regards to a religion). Thats four things PEOPLE deal out enough of.<br>
<br>
I believe in love. <img src="images/smilies/wink.gif" border=0> I believe in preternatural things (ha! didnt use the super- version Prof., teehehe). And I belive in freedom. I suppose I am agnostic, however I hate to use that word, as it has been butt givled across the net for years now (present company excluded).<br>
<br>
I grew up with both non-denominational Christian and secular Judaism teachings (my mother is jewish). I enjoyed both I suppose, never had anything shoved down my throat and banged into my head at a young age. Around 13 or 14, I kinda lost both religions and didnt reaquaint until 19 or 20. I'm still not that religious of a person, I dont go to gatherings, I dont read the books. But I always liked to have that 'faith'. And I dont think it ever has been, or will be a weakness. Its just something I carry with me. Something I really do enjoy having, needing, experincing, and possibly sharing...<br>
<br>
Its very hard for me to explain I suppose. On one hand its hard to define, and on the other, well come on...I draw stick figures in MS Paint. You guys are a lot smarter, and sharper with the words than me. <img src="images/smilies/smile.gif" border=0><br>
<br>
But let me reiterate my first point. All the 'bad' things about religions we can almost all agree on, I'm against. Religions have brought a lot of problems into the world, and still do. They also do good. And thats what I try to seperate. I completely understand why some people are driven from religions. Take a simple look around the world, and anyone could understand.<br>
<br>
Kith</font></p>

